// D&D's Spike Growth deals piercing damage AND turns the zone into difficult terrain for
// anyone who moves through it, for the whole Concentration duration. `GroundAoe` cannot deliver
// both halves at once: `pooled_debuff`, when set, replaces the normal `damage`/`poise`/
// `knockback` strike entirely (see `CharacterAbility::GroundAoe`'s own doc comment) -- it is one
// or the other, not both. The difficult-terrain half is the spell's namesake and its actually
// distinctive rider (plain piercing damage already exists on a dozen other spells), so that's
// the half kept; the piercing-damage-per-step rider is dropped rather than half-modeled.
//
// `pooled_debuff` is a shared-HP-budget mechanism built for `Sleep`'s "weakest targets first,
// until the budget runs out" semantics (`combat::resolve_pooled_debuff_targets`) -- there is no
// ungated "apply this buff to everyone in the radius" GroundAoe path in this engine. `pool` is
// set generously above any plausible in-radius total HP at this level band so the budget
// realistically never runs out before every target in the 6m sphere is caught, approximating
// "anyone in the zone" rather than gating to the weakest few. Tune once real mob HP bands are
// measured against it (same explicit caveat as sleep.ron's own pool value).
GroundAoe(
    energy_cost: 65.0,
    buildup_duration: 0.6,
    delay: 0.5,
    recover_duration: 0.4,
    max_range: 46.0,
    radius: 6.0,
    min_falloff: 0.4,
    damage: 0.0,
    poise: 0.0,
    knockback: (strength: 0.0, direction: Away),
    dodgeable: No,
    reagent: None,
    rooted_cast: false,
    pooled_debuff: Some((
        pool: 70.0,
        buff: (
            kind: DifficultTerrain,
            dur_secs: 60,
            strength: Value(0.6),
            chance: 1.0,
        ),
    )),
    meta: (school: Some(Transmutation), source: Some(Primordial), cooldown: Some(20.0)),
)
